title: Blinking LEDs
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-01-01.png
  - .assets/img-101-01-02.png
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-01-03.png

steps:

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

        {[ht,$start]%[cu]BlinkerP% &ndash; Blinking LEDs}

        Having completed the **Orientation** tours, you've already viewed (and executed) the {[cu]BlinkerP} program.

        In fact, you also know quite about the other **Units** used by this program &ndash; {[cu]BoardC}, {[cu]Common}, {[cu]AppLed}.

        {[in]We will highlight some other <b>EMS</b> features seen here.}

        To prepare for other **Basic Examples** tours, we'll now pivot to *real-time analysis* [RTA] of **EMS** programs.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 4
      - mark 1 B 7
      - mark 1 C 9
      - mark 1 D 11
      - mark 1 E 12
    focus: [1,1]
    acts: ['$build']
    text: |

        {[ht,$details]%[cu]BlinkerP% &ndash; A Few Details}

        More language features you've noticed along the way:

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,A]} The {[ck]import} of {[cu]BoardC} uses {[cs]'@$distro'} to logically name the **Bucket** associated with the current &emsp;&nbsp; <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>.

        {[bm,B]} Like {[ck]import}, {[cs]$delegate} brings a **Unit** into scope &ndash; but without knowing its `'@bucket/Unit.em'` name.

        {[bm,C]} The {[cs]em$run} intrinsic provides a &laquo;`main`&raquo; program entry-point &ndash; executed after any {[cs]em$startup} calls.

        {[bm,D]} Local identifiers starting with `_` conventionally im&shy;ply their *non-usage* &ndash; silencing some **TS** warnings.

        {[bm,E]} Numeric constants can use `_` for readability &ndash; part of standard **TS** automatically inherited by **EMS**.

        </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

        {[ht,browse_activity]&thinsp;Introducing PulseView}

        The **Basic Examples** tours will leverage **PulseView** for real-time analysis [RTA] of **EMS** programs.

        {[in]The <b>EM&bull;Home</b> editor tab contains a link for installing this application on your PC.}

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,1]} When running, the **Wokwi LOGIC** block wired to our simulated **Pi Pico** board captures signals **D0** - **D7**.

        {[bm,2]} Terminating the simulator with its **Stop ■** button saves a special `wokwi.vcd` file into your workspace.

        {[bm,3]} Reloading `wokwi.vcd` inside a running **PulseView** window will refresh the application's display.

        </div>

        {[in]To zoom the image in&middot;out, press <b>Ctrl</b> (or <b>Cmd</b>) within its editor tab while scrolling your mouse down&middot;up.}

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |

        {[ht,checklist]&thinsp;PulseView Setup}

        You should first **build&middot;load** {[cu]BlinkerP} and then explictly terminate the **Wokwi** Simulator using its **Stop ■** button.

        {[in]Status messages will pop-up in the lower-right corner of your <b>VS Code</b> window.&thinsp; Go <b>Back</b> for reference.}

        You'll now import `$HOME/EM/repo/emporium/wokwi.vcd` after launching the **PulseView** application.

        {[bm,A]} select **Import Value Change ...** to locate this file<br>
        {[bm,B]} set **Downsampling** to **125** before clicking **Ok**<br>
        {[bm,C]} drag the **UART** signal above the **D0** signal

        Feel free to keep **PulseView** active while working with other programs on these tours.

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 26
      - mark 4 B 57

    focus: [4,30]
    acts: ['$reveal']
    text: |

        {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% &ndash; Closer Look}

        Signals **D0 - D7** first captured by **Wokwi** and then ren&shy;dered by **PulseView** have their origin within {[cu]BoardC}.

        Starting at line {[bm,A]}, {[cu]BoardC} uses {[cs]$clone} to synthesize these (and other) supporting **Modules**: 

        &nbsp;&nbsp;**D0**&thinsp;{[cu]AppOutPin}|&thinsp;**D1**&thinsp;{[cu]DbgA}|&thinsp;**D2**&thinsp;{[cu]DbgB}|&thinsp;**D3**&thinsp;{[cu]DbgC}|&thinsp;**D4**&thinsp;{[cu]DbgD}<br>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;**D5**&thinsp;{[cu]AppButPin}|&thinsp;**D6**&thinsp;{[cu]AppLedPin}|&thinsp;**D7**&thinsp;{[cu]SysLedPin}

        Each implementing the {[cu]GpioI} **Interface**, {[cs]em$configure} ultimately binds their {[cn]pin_num} {[cs]$config} parameters.

        The {[cs]$board} intrinsic at line {[bm,B]} returns a helper object popu&shy;lated by reading a collocated `em-boards` file.

        {[in]You'll always find this file in the <code class="cmd-cs">'@$distro'</code> <b>Bucket</b>.}

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |

        {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp;%[cu]BlinkerP% &ndash; Signal Capture}

        At this point, we'll examine the values of signals **D0 - D7** over five seconds of {[cu]BlinkerP} execution time.

        Label {[bm,A]} shows two quick winks of the [RED] `SysLed`, which **EMS** will always trigger before entering {[cs]em$run}.

        Label {[bm,B]} uses the **PulseView** cursor to measure the ac&shy;curacy of {[cu]Common}.{[cn]BusyWait} over {[ce]500_000} &mu;seconds.

        Label {[bm,C]} show a special {[ce]0x4} byte written to `AppOut` through {[cd]halt}, followed by asserting `SysLed`.

        {[in]If not called explicitly, the <b>EMS</b> run-time automati&shy;cally invokes <code class="cmd-cd">halt</code> during orderly program shutdown.}

        The next tour shows what happens when calling {[cd]fail}.

  - cmds:
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

        {[ht,$extra]Extra Credit}

        {[bi,task_alt]} Come up to speed on the **PulseView** UI.

        {[bi,task_alt]} Inspect the **UART** signal after running {[cu]HelloP}.



        {[bi,task_alt]} View captures of your own `zgit.*` programs.

  - cmds:
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

        {[ht,$steps]Next Steps}

        The **Basic Examples** tours will present many variants of {[cu]BlinkerP} &ndash; each blinking LEDS, albeit very differently.

        Along the way, we'll visit some additional (portable) **Units** found in the `em.core` **Package**.

        We'll also introduce a few more **EMS** features &ndash; though you've actually seen much of the language already.

        The truly novel aspect of these tours lies in leverag&shy;ing **PulseView** for analyzing real-time program execution.

        {[hc]}